Subject: [PATCH] imx219: let 6 sensors safely share one reset GPIO (Auvidea J106)

On the Auvidea J106 carrier all 6 IMX219 CSI cameras share a SINGLE physical
reset line (TEGRA_MAIN_GPIO(R,5) = global sysfs gpio 461), released at boot by a
gpio-hog. The stock tegracam imx219 driver assumes a PRIVATE per-sensor reset:
it gpio_request()s the line EXCLUSIVELY, frees it on power_put, and drives it LOW
(asserts reset) on every power_on and power_off. With one shared line that breaks
two ways:

  1. sensors 2..6 fail probe with -EBUSY/-16 (the line is already owned), which
     is why the device tree previously needed per-sensor *dummy* reset pins; and
  2. even once probed, opening, closing or crashing ONE sensor would drive the
     shared line low and reset ALL the others mid-stream.

This mirrors how the Auvidea TX1 R24.2.1 BSP solved it (one shared reset,
released once at boot and NEVER asserted per-sensor). With this patch all 6
sensors point reset-gpios at the one real line (461) and the driver:

  - treats -EBUSY from gpio_request() as success, so a sibling sensor (or the
    gpio-hog) that already owns the shared line does not fail probe;
  - never frees the shared reset line, so unbinding one sensor cannot release
    reset for the others (or free a descriptor it never owned);
  - never drives the shared line LOW in power_on or power_off, so starting,
    stopping or losing one sensor never resets the others. The line is brought
    high (released) once and left there; IMX219 re-init relies on its I2C
    software reset (reg 0x0103), exactly like the TX1 driver.

Net effect: the 6 cameras behave as independent devices that merely share a
reset wire - a missing, stopped or crashed sensor leaves the rest streaming.

Target: L4T R32.7.6 (kernel 4.9.337), drivers/media/i2c/imx219.c.
Apply from the kernel source root (the dir that contains kernel/nvidia):
    patch -p1 < patches/0001-imx219-share-reset-gpio-j106.patch

--- a/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx219.c
+++ b/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx219.c
@@ -314,12 +314,10 @@
 		return err;
 	}
 
-	if (pw->reset_gpio) {
-		if (gpio_cansleep(pw->reset_gpio))
-			gpio_set_value_cansleep(pw->reset_gpio, 0);
-		else
-			gpio_set_value(pw->reset_gpio, 0);
-	}
+	/* J106: the reset line is SHARED by all 6 IMX219 and is released once at
+	 * boot by a gpio-hog. Do NOT assert it low here; pulsing reset on a
+	 * power_on would reset every other (possibly streaming) sensor. Mirrors
+	 * the Auvidea TX1 R24.2.1 driver ("release once, never assert"). */
 
 	if (unlikely(!(pw->avdd || pw->iovdd || pw->dvdd)))
 		goto skip_power_seqn;
@@ -390,12 +388,8 @@
 			return err;
 		}
 	} else {
-		if (pw->reset_gpio) {
-			if (gpio_cansleep(pw->reset_gpio))
-				gpio_set_value_cansleep(pw->reset_gpio, 0);
-			else
-				gpio_set_value(pw->reset_gpio, 0);
-		}
+		/* J106: shared reset - never assert low on power_off, otherwise
+		 * stopping/closing one sensor would reset all the others. (TX1-style.) */
 
 		usleep_range(10, 10);
 
@@ -433,8 +427,9 @@
 	pw->avdd = NULL;
 	pw->iovdd = NULL;
 
-	if (likely(pw->reset_gpio))
-		gpio_free(pw->reset_gpio);
+	/* J106: reset line is shared by all 6 sensors - never free it here,
+	 * otherwise unbinding one sensor would release reset for the others
+	 * (or free a descriptor this instance never owned). */
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -489,9 +484,14 @@
 		goto done;
 	}
 
-	/* Reset or ENABLE GPIO */
+	/* Reset or ENABLE GPIO.
+	 * Auvidea J106: all 6 IMX219 share ONE physical reset line, so the
+	 * gpio may already be owned by a sibling sensor or by a gpio-hog
+	 * (-EBUSY). Treat that as success and just drive the shared line. */
 	pw->reset_gpio = pdata->reset_gpio;
 	err = gpio_request(pw->reset_gpio, "cam_reset_gpio");
+	if (err == -EBUSY)
+		err = 0;
 	if (err < 0) {
 		dev_err(dev, "%s: unable to request reset_gpio (%d)\n",
 			__func__, err);
